Durarara!! AU
Chapter 17 – Part 2 
Curtis didn’t look away from the window when he answered.
“You sure about that?” His voice stayed flat. “Try digging some dirt on me. Izaya can’t. Meaning the people I’ve erased stayed erased. No problem would have come from this.”
A short pause. The city lights slid across his face in broken lines.
“I am glad Kaz is safe. He seems like a good guy.” Another beat, quieter but no less firm. “For me, immigrants aren’t bad people. Some of them are just trying to get away from bad situations.”
The van held that for a moment.
Walker let out a low breath.
“…He just casually confirmed body-disposal success rate and human empathy in the same breath. That’s a hell of a combination.”
Erika’s eyes narrowed slightly, not in hostility — in recalibration.
“If Izaya can’t find your leftovers, that’s not normal. That’s a skill set.”
Saburo kept his eyes on the road.
“Great. So the guy we pulled off the sidewalk is both anti-trafficking and professionally untraceable. Love that for us.”
Kadota leaned back, studying Curtis with a longer, heavier silence than before. The practical worry was still there, but it had been joined by something closer to respect — the kind given to people who had survived systems designed to erase others.
“You’re saying two things at once,” Kadota said. “That you can make men like Asanuma disappear without consequence… and that the people they prey on deserve the kind of protection most of the city never bothers giving them.”
A short nod.
“I can live with that combination longer than I can live with the opposite.” He glanced toward Kaztano’s direction in the residual space of the night’s rescue. “Kaz is safe. That’s the win. And for what it’s worth… I agree with you. Most of the ones getting hunted out here aren’t villains. They’re just convenient.”
Walker looked between them, then muttered,
“Still not over the fact that even Izaya hit a wall on him. That’s terrifying in a cool way.”
Curtis finally glanced back from the window, mismatched eyes calm.
“Cool is one word for it.”
Curtis leaned his head back against the seat, long black braids shifting over the black technical hoodie as a faint, dry note entered his voice.
“If Izaya approaches any of you for info on me, make sure he makes it worth your while. I named my price with him. Something he can’t even afford.”
Kadota’s eyes narrowed slightly.
“What’s that?”
Curtis didn’t smile so much as let the answer sit there like a closed door.
“Friendship.”
For a second, the van just processed it.
Walker blinked.
“…I’m sorry. Your price was friendship?”
Erika’s mouth opened, then curved into pure delighted disbelief.
“He charged Izaya Orihara, information broker of the damned, emotional intimacy as currency. That’s either the most beautiful refusal I’ve ever heard or the most psychologically violent one.”
Saburo snorted from the driver’s seat.
“No wonder the flea couldn’t pay. He doesn’t stock that.”
Kadota actually huffed once — almost a laugh, almost not — and shook his head.
“That’s a hell of a wall to put in front of a man like him.” A short pause. “Also smart. Money he’d understand. Favors he’d route around. Threats he’d enjoy. Friendship forces him into a language he doesn’t speak.”
Walker leaned forward, eyes shining.
“Please tell me he hated it.”
Curtis looked toward the window again, mismatched eyes half-lidded.
“He found it refreshing. Which is worse.”
Erika put a hand over her heart like she was witnessing history.
“We pulled a man into the van this morning and he is already out here economically destroying Izaya with emotional availability. I love this city.”
Curtis sat with the quiet for a second longer, long black braids resting against the black technical hoodie, mismatched eyes lowered toward the van floor.
“So I may be cold in some ways. But I’m not evil. I’ve just been exposed frequently to a different environment.” A short pause. The edge in his voice softened, almost imperceptibly. “To be honest, it kinda hurts that I’m treated like a problem. But it’s fine.”
He looked up, already reaching for the door handle.
“Anyways. Toodles~”
The side door slammed open to wind and street noise.
“Nightstar—!”
Kadota lunged too late. Walker shouted. Erika made a sound that was half gasp, half scandalized delight. Saburo swore and jerked the wheel on reflex as Curtis rolled out of the moving van in one clean, practiced motion, hit the pavement in a controlled tumble, and came up running. By the time any of them got a clear look through the open door, the black technical silhouette and long braids had already vanished into a break between buildings.
Saburo yanked the van toward the curb hard enough to make the tires complain.
“Did he just toodles out of a moving vehicle?!”
Walker was practically hanging out the side.
“He did. He absolutely did. That was an exit. That was a full narrative exit.”
Erika stared into the alley mouth where Curtis had disappeared, eyes wide.
“He confessed cartel background, priced Izaya out of friendship, got mildly emotionally wounded, said toodles, and then left the van like a video game cutscene. I can’t keep up.”
Kadota dragged a hand down his face, then looked at the empty stretch of street with a hard, reluctant respect.
“…Of course he can disappear too.”
Saburo slammed the door shut again, furious.
“If he scratched my paint with that stunt, I’m billing the whole city.”
The van sat there for a second longer in the aftermath, four people staring at the space where their temporary problem — helpful, cold, untraceable, and strangely soft underneath — had simply removed himself from the board.
Walker finally sat back, breathless.
“…We’re never getting a normal day back, are we?”
Kadota didn’t answer.
He was still looking at the alley.
They found him again two hours later.
Not because he wanted to be found — because Ikebukuro was only so large when people like Kadota actually started looking. Curtis was on a side street near a convenience store, black technical hoodie dusty at the sleeve from the earlier roll-out, long black braids neat again, buying a canned coffee like he had not just left a moving van in the middle of a conversation.
The van pulled up. This time the door opened more carefully.
Walker leaned out first.
“You absolute menace. You don’t get to say toodles, leave a moving vehicle, and end the scene.”
Erika pointed at him.
“We still have unfinished category work.”
Curtis cracked the coffee open, mismatched eyes half-lidded.
“…You four are persistent.”
Kadota stepped out properly this time, hands in his pockets, no theatrics.
“We are. Get in. Or talk out here. Either way, we’re finishing the part you skipped by throwing yourself into traffic.”
Curtis looked at them for a second, then leaned against the storefront wall instead of entering the van. Boundary already visible in the posture.
“Fine. Talk.”

They soft-recruited him the only way people like them knew how.
Walker and Erika wanted the myth:
•  the foreigner who could text the Black Rider
•  the man even Izaya couldn’t dig up
•  the one who rolled out of vans and talked about ditches like weather
Kadota wanted reliability:
•  someone who showed up when people were taken
•  someone who did not freeze
•  someone who could be called when the next ugly thing crawled into the open
Saburo wanted fewer unknowns:
•  preferably none
•  especially none that could damage his paint
Curtis heard all of it, drank his coffee, and then drew the line cleanly.
“I have access to the Dollars site and chat. That doesn’t make me one of you.” His voice stayed even. “I don’t consider myself Dollars. I won’t take orders from a crowd or a color. If people are being preyed on — trafficked, hunted, sold — I’ll help. That’s the extent of it.”
A short pause.
“I’m not your member. I’m not your weapon. I’m not your ongoing problem to assign a label to. I’m a man who helped once and may help again if the reason is good.”
The refusal should have soured the air. Instead, it settled into something more complicated.
Kadota nodded once, slow and accepting.
“Clear enough.”
Walker looked heartbroken and impressed at the same time.
“So near to party membership and yet so far.”
Erika smiled despite herself.
“He’s hotter when he sets terms. Annoying, but hotter.”
Saburo pointed at him over the steering wheel.
“If you ever jump out of my van again, I’m billing you for emotional damages.”
Curtis raised the coffee can slightly in acknowledgment.
“No promises.”

The fallout started before the evening finished.
Masaomi heard enough through the van gang’s after-circuit to go quiet in the particular way he did when a threat assessment changed shape. Curtis was no longer only a sharp transfer student with bad timing around Mikado. He was connected now — to Kadota’s crew, to a trafficking rescue, to the kind of darkness Masaomi had spent years trying to keep smaller kids from treating like adventure. Useful. Hazardous. Too close.
Mikado received the information in fragments and felt the jealousy harden into something denser. Curtis was not only good with Anri. Not only calm in hallways. Not only the one people looked at when things went wrong. He was being pulled into the older circle, the dangerous circle, the place Mikado still only romanticized from the outside. Every new connection around Curtis made Mikado feel one step further from the center of his own city story.
Anri heard that he had helped pull someone out of a trafficking hold and felt the same quiet pull as before — only deeper. Another version of his protectiveness. Another proof that when people were being broken by stronger systems, he moved toward them instead of away. She did not need the details to understand the shape of it. She saved the feeling the same way she had saved his number.

Celty’s private thread did not stay quiet either.
Later, after the lot and the yellow helmet’s half-recognition, after Shooter had carried her away from the industrial edge-space, her message landed on Curtis’s phone with less distance than before.
Celty:
You were there.
With Kadota’s people.
Celty:
You move like someone I’ve seen before.
Celty:
If I ask you directly whether that was only you today…
would you answer?
Curtis stared at the message for a long time outside the convenience store after the van finally left him alone.
He did not answer immediately.
Across Ikebukuro, the first half of the problem had finished arranging itself:
•  the van gang had bonded to him without owning him
•  the Dollars remained a door he could open but refused to live inside
•  Masaomi’s caution had sharpened
•  Mikado’s jealousy had deepened
•  Anri had stepped closer
•  and Celty was beginning to test the man in her phone against the rider in the street
Curtis finished the coffee, long black braids shifting in the evening air, and started walking again.
He was still claimed by no one.
That was becoming its own kind of danger.
Curtis looked down at his phone, read Celty’s latest messages once, and then exhaled the word like it had been punched out of him.
“Fuuuuuck.”
The van gang all turned at the same time.
Walker’s eyebrows shot up.
“That’s not a casual fuck. That’s a life-consequences fuck.”
Erika leaned in immediately.
“Who texted you. Was it the Black Rider. Was it Izaya. Was it an ex. Rank the disaster.”
Saburo eyed him from the driver’s side like Curtis might roll out into traffic again at any second.
“If that message is about to become my problem, I want warning.”
Kadota just watched him, waiting.
Curtis locked the phone and slipped it back into his pocket in one clean motion, long black braids shifting as he shook his head once.
“It’s nothing.”
Walker stared.
“Bro. Nobody says fuuuuuck like that about nothing.”
Erika nodded hard.
“That was the sound of a man watching several plotlines make eye contact.”
Curtis already started walking, black technical hoodie blending into the evening foot traffic.
“It’s nothing,” he repeated, flatter this time. “Don’t worry about it.”
None of them looked convinced.
Which was fair.
Because on the locked screen in his pocket, Celty’s question was still sitting there unanswered — and it was very much not nothing.
Curtis didn’t stop walking. He only glanced back over his shoulder, mismatched eyes dry as dust, and delivered the lie with perfect deadpan sarcasm.
“My ex wants me back in L.A. Guess I’m gone in a few days.”
The van gang reacted in four completely different wrong directions at once.
Walker clutched his chest.
“NO. You can’t leave the story after one arc. That’s criminal pacing.”
Erika looked personally betrayed.
“If you disappear to America after we finally got you categorized, I will start a forum thread so toxic it becomes a local hazard.”
Saburo brightened despite himself.
“…Wait. If he’s leaving, does that mean no more random van exits?”
Kadota did not buy it for a second. His eyes narrowed, practical and unamused.
“You’re lying.”
Curtis gave the smallest shrug and kept going, long black braids shifting against the black technical hoodie as the evening crowd started to take him.
“Am I?”
Walker called after him,
“That wasn’t a denial!”
Erika cupped her hands around her mouth.
“If you actually leave, I’m making Walker write your unfinished character route!”
Curtis lifted one hand in a lazy half-wave without turning around, already vanishing into the street like the conversation had reached the exact amount of explanation he was willing to pay.
Behind him, Kadota watched the space he left and muttered,
“…Yeah. He’s not going to L.A.”
Walker nodded grimly.
“No chance. A man does not fuuuuuck at his phone like that over an ex. That’s current-city disaster energy.”
Curtis slowed just enough for the words to carry back to them, long black braids shifting as he spoke without fully turning around.
“My ex was pretty bad. But no — just some ongoing things that are not street problems. Personal ones.”
That landed differently than the L.A. joke.
Walker’s expression cooled from theatrical panic into actual curiosity.
“…Personal as in emotional-personal, or personal as in ‘if we ask more he’ll disappear into another alley’ personal?”
Erika studied his back like she was trying to read the unanswered text through his hoodie.
“Because those are very different categories. One gets sympathy. The other gets a search party and a corkboard.”
Saburo snorted quietly.
“If it’s personal, leave it. Every time people in this city say ‘it’s personal,’ someone ends up bleeding or married.”
Kadota didn’t push hard. He just filed the distinction away with the rest of what he’d learned about Curtis since morning.
“Personal means we don’t get to demand the details.” A short pause. “It also means you should be careful. Street problems are simple. Personal ones follow you home.”
Curtis gave a small acknowledging sound, already walking again.
“Yeah. I noticed.”
He raised the half-finished coffee can once in a vague parting gesture and let the crowd take him, leaving the van gang with a cleaner truth than before — not a random disaster, not an ex flying him back to California, just a man carrying private complications that the street could not solve for him.
Curtis glanced back one last time, mismatched eyes half-lidded with mild mischief under the streetlights.
“Ladies problems. You get it, right, Walker and Erika?” A short beat. “You guys are a couple. So this happens, right?”
Walker made a sound like a dying engine.
“WE— WE ARE NOT—”
Erika choked so hard she actually had to hit her own chest.
“STOP SAYING THAT LIKE IT’S ESTABLISHED CANON—”
That was all the opening Curtis needed.
By the time either of them recovered enough air to form a full sentence, the black technical hoodie and long braids had already slipped into the denser foot traffic and vanished between bodies, storefronts, and evening neon. Gone clean.
Kadota watched the empty space he left and actually laughed — a short, real sound.
“He did it again.”
Saburo was already grinning in the driver’s seat.
“Every single time. One couple comment and those two blue-screen like cheap laptops. He’s got them solved.”
Walker pointed furiously at the crowd, still red.
“That man is a menace to society!”
Erika, still flustered, glared at the alley he had used.
“He is using emotional warfare. Illegal. Immoral. Effective.”
Kadota shook his head and opened the van door again.
“Come on. Let him go. If he’s got ladies problems, that’s one category of trouble I’m happy to leave uninvestigated.”
Saburo pulled away from the curb, still chuckling.
“Speak for yourself. I want front-row seats if Walker and Erika ever actually explode.”
Behind them, Ikebukuro swallowed Curtis whole again — one more clean exit, one more unfinished personal thread still vibrating in his pocket.
Walker recovered enough air to turn on Erika, still flushed but already grinning like a man seizing revenge.
“He glazed over your hot comment earlier. Or didn’t hear it.”
Erika’s head snapped toward him so fast her hair almost followed a frame late.
“Excuse me?”
Walker held up both hands, still laughing.
“‘He’s hotter when he sets terms’? That was you. Full send. And he just kept talking like you were reading the weather report. Either he didn’t catch it, or he caught it and chose peace.”
Erika’s face went pink all over again.
“I was making a character analysis observation. It was academic.”
Saburo snorted from the front.
“Academic my ass.”
Kadota didn’t even look back.
“It was not academic.”
Erika pointed at all of them in turn, mortified and furious.
“I am allowed to notice narrative magnetism. That does not mean I got ignored by a braided final boss and need to be roasted for it in traffic.”
Walker leaned toward the window, still cackling.
“He absolutely heard it. Man said toodles, jumped out of a van, came back, set boundaries, and still didn’t even give you a reaction point. That’s cold.”
Erika sank an inch lower in her seat, arms crossed tight.
“…I hate this friend group.”
Saburo smiled at the road ahead.
“No you don’t. You hate that he didn’t flinch.”
Kadota exhaled through his nose, almost amused.
“Drive, Saburo. Before she makes Walker write a redemption fic about it.”
Kadota finally spoke over the front of the seat, cutting through the teasing with the same practical tone he used for everything that actually mattered.
“Maybe he doesn’t want to date. Hence ‘ladies problems.’ And he did mention his ex was pretty bad.”
The van quieted a notch.
Walker’s grin faltered into something more thoughtful.
“…Oh. So not ‘multiple women fighting over him’ problems. More like ‘people get close and it becomes a mess he doesn’t want’ problems.”
Erika’s embarrassment cooled into consideration.
“That… actually tracks. He helps. He listens. He shows up. Then the second it starts looking like attachment, he does the vanishing act. Ex was bad, so now even normal interest feels like a trap.”
Saburo nodded once, eyes on the road.
“Seen that before. Guys who’ll walk into a knife fight no problem but treat a sincere text like live explosives.”
Kadota leaned an arm against the door, watching the street where Curtis had disappeared.
“He’s not cold because he doesn’t feel anything. He’s careful because he already knows what it costs when the wrong person gets in close.” A short pause. “Doesn’t mean people won’t keep trying. Just means he’s going to keep setting that same boundary until someone either respects it… or becomes the next problem he walks away from.”
Walker sat back, processing.
“So my man is out here carrying cartel trauma, untraceable body disposal ethics, Black Rider texts, and dating aversion.” A beat. “He’s not a route. He’s a whole unfinished saga.”
Erika sighed, less theatrical now.
“…Yeah. Suddenly ‘hotter when he sets terms’ feels less like a joke and more like a warning label.”
Kadota glanced back just long enough to deliver it flat.
“Yeah. Be careful making your passes at him — or ‘character analysis,’ as you call it. He seems to run from affection.”
The van lasted about one full second before it broke.
Walker cracked first, laughing hard enough to tip sideways in his seat.
“DOTACHIN JUST SAID IT OUT LOUD—”
Erika made a strangled noise that turned into laughter despite herself.
“I WAS BEING ACADEMIC—”
“You were being thirsty with vocabulary,” Saburo cut in, already grinning at the windshield.
Kadota shook his head, but even he couldn’t fully kill the smile pulling at the edge of his mouth.
“I’m serious. Man helps with traffickers, quotes cartel logic, then disappears the second anyone gets too warm. You keep poking that and he’s going to start treating compliments like incoming fire.”
Walker wiped at one eye, still laughing.
“Imagine trying to flirt and watching him open the door mid-conversation again. Toodles. Gone.”
Erika slumped back, laughing and defeated.
“I hate that you’re probably right.”
The van rolled on through the evening traffic, four people laughing at the shape of a man who could face down the ugliest parts of the city without blinking — and still treat genuine affection like a hazard to be exited cleanly.
Away from the van, away from the laughter and the labels, Curtis stopped under a quieter stretch of streetlight and flipped his phone open.
Celty’s messages were still there, unanswered and precise.
You were there.
With Kadota’s people.
You move like someone I’ve seen before.
If I ask you directly whether that was only you today… would you answer?
He stared at them long enough for the screen to dim once, then woke it again and typed.
Curtis:
Yes.
I was there.
A short pause. Then more.
Curtis:
I told them I could text you.
They needed the approach coordinated.
That’s all.
Another beat. His thumb hovered before the last part.
Curtis:
If you’re asking whether it was only me today…
I’m not answering that one yet.
He sent it, locked the screen, and leaned back against the nearest wall, long black braids shifting over the black technical hoodie as the evening air cooled around him.
Not a full confession.
Not a clean denial.
Just enough truth to keep the private thread from becoming a lie — and enough silence to keep the sealed helmet from becoming public.
Across the city, the message would land in Celty’s hands like a door left only slightly open.
The reply came faster than he expected.
Celty:
“Not answering that one yet” is still an answer.
A pause.
Celty:
You were there.
You can text me.
You move like the rider from the other night.
And now you’re choosing which questions get daylight.
Another message followed, tighter.
Celty:
I’m not asking as the Black Rider.
I’m asking as the person who was texting you while that race was still in my head.
A longer pause, then one last line:
Celty:
If there are two of you, say so.
If there’s one, stop making me split you into pieces.
On the quiet sidewalk, Curtis stared at the screen, the glow cutting across his mismatched eyes. The boundary he had just drawn with the van gang felt simple compared to this. Street problems had edges. This one kept asking him to name himself out loud.